                "entry_id": "419694a6-ecaf-3454-8cc2-18734dc72130",
                "word": "chlorocruorin",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "fe109d8a-f8b9-37ea-868b-ad868b34783d",
                        "entry_id": "419694a6-ecaf-3454-8cc2-18734dc72130",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A green respiratory pigment found in many annelids."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "uncountable"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "c6e6a121-876f-4f84-b0dd-936e56dbe3fb",
                                "sense_id": "fe109d8a-f8b9-37ea-868b-ad868b34783d",
                                "text": "Later it was shown that the α-bands of the pyridine haemochromogens of both cytochrome a and chlorocruorin resemble each other closely (Keilin, 1933 a)."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "504bc246-9202-4a39-8dd7-7148f36acf32",
                                "sense_id": "fe109d8a-f8b9-37ea-868b-ad868b34783d",
                                "text": "The affinity of chlorocruorin for oxygen is equivalent to the haemoglobin. It is interesting that within the same family of worms like [S]abellidae, [S]erpulidae and [A]mphotritidae same^([sic]) species have chlorocruorin while others have haemoglobin."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "257ea6fd-f378-47f3-bcdf-5a5c9f6f661f",
                                "sense_id": "fe109d8a-f8b9-37ea-868b-ad868b34783d",
                                "text": "1992, N. B. Terwilliger, Chapter 8: Molecular Structure of the Extracellular Heme Proteins, Ch. P. Mangum (editor), Advances in Comparative & Environmental Physiology 13: Blood and Tissue Oxygen Carriers, Springer-Verlag, 1992 softcover reprint, page 203,\nIt was earlier thought that chlorocruorin was very different from hemoglobin based on a subunit structure with a mass of 34-35 kDa (Antonini et al. 1962; Guerratore and Zito 1977). […] Some writers have questioned the value of drawing a distinction between the chlorocruorins and hemoglobins, especially from an evolutionary point of view (Mangum 1985; see also Toulson, Chap. 9, this Vol.). The visual phenomenon of green blood has perhaps led to a stronger distinction between chlorocruorin and hemoglobin than warranted."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
